# =============================================================================
# DeepSeek-V4-Flash MXFP4 at 131072 -- KV in RAM, experts in VRAM, repacked
# =============================================================================
# Same model, same quant, same context as the `deepseek-v4-flash` profile. What
# differs is where things live, and it is worth a lot on prefill.
#
#   MEASURED at 32k prompt depth, against what that profile ships (RESULTS §11):
#     --fit, KV on GPU, ub 512     287.1 pp / 19.75 tg
#     this profile, ub 2048        484.1 pp / 20.98 tg      +69 % / +6 %
#     this profile as shipped      462.9 pp / 20.07 tg      +61 % / +2 %
#   Nothing is traded on quality: identical MXFP4 weights, f16 KV, no -ser. The
#   shipped row is -ub 1024 rather than 2048 -- see IK_UBATCH below, it is a
#   stability decision and costs ~4 % of prefill.
#
# Three stacked reasons, all placement or scheduling, none of them numerical:
#
#   1. -rtr repacks the CPU-resident experts into the row-interleaved layout the
#      AVX2/VNNI kernels want ("Repacked 60 tensors"). Worth +25 % alone. This is
#      the same trick as the step-3.7-flash-q4-r4 profile (§1.6/§1.7), just done
#      at load time instead of baked into the file. It also shrinks the CUDA
#      compute buffer, which is why --n-cpu-moe 17 fits at all (TODO item 10).
#   2. -nkvo moves the KV to host memory, and the freed VRAM is spent on experts
#      via an explicit --n-cpu-moe. 17 fits and beats 18 on both axes.
#   3. Because the attention scratch follows the KV to the host, the CUDA compute
#      buffer collapses from 3624 MiB to 440 MiB -- which is what makes -ub 2048
#      affordable at all. Under --fit, -ub 1024 does not merely fail to fit, it
#      ABORTS in cublas_handle. This step is worth another +20 %.
#
#   ./serve.sh deepseek-v4-flash-128k-kvram
#   ./serve-deepseek-v4-flash-mxfp4-kvram-128k.sh
#
# WHEN NOT TO USE THIS. Since 2026-08-12 this is the toolkit's default profile
# (config/default.env), but it is less robust than the --fit profile:
#   * placement is manual, so it does not adapt -- and it leaves only ~1.6 GiB of
#     VRAM free. Anything else touching the card (a desktop compositor, LM Studio
#     on this GPU) will break the load.
#   * -rtr forces --no-mmap, so every start re-reads the model: ~30 s from a warm
#     page cache here, minutes from cold.
# For a service that simply has to come up -- the Hermes fallback, say -- prefer
# serve-deepseek-v4-flash-mxfp4-gpu-cpu-128k.sh.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Same MXFP4 quant as the deepseek-v4-flash profile (~145.6 GiB, 4-file split).
: "${IK_MODEL_GLOB:=${IK_MODELS_ROOT}/lmstudio-community/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-GGUF/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-MXFP4-00001-of-*.gguf}"

: "${IK_ALIAS:=deepseek-v4-flash-128k-kvram}"
: "${IK_CTX:=131072}"

# --- placement: manual, and --fit would actively get in the way -------------
# --fit subtracts the KV size from the VRAM it thinks it has even when -nkvo
# means the KV is never allocated there. Under it, -nkvo alone changes the
# weight split by exactly nothing and leaves the freed VRAM idle (VERIFIED at
# 512k, §10.2). Hence the explicit split.
#
# 17 is the ceiling at -ub 2048, and only WITH -rtr: it lands at 96 238 MiB of
# 97 887 used. --n-cpu-moe 16 OOMs.
#
# The dependence on -rtr is not obvious and cost a segfault to find (2026-08-13,
# TODO item 10). Across 9 runs the weight buffers are byte-identical either way
# (92 630.93 MiB CUDA0 / 56 498.00 MiB host), but the CUDA compute buffer is
# 1760.01 MiB with -rtr and 2496.01 MiB without. Those 736 MiB are most of this
# profile's headroom, so without -rtr the second logical batch (p0=4096) cannot
# re-reserve the buffer and cudaMalloc fails. If -rtr is ever turned off here,
# --n-cpu-moe must go to 18 with it.
: "${IK_FIT:=0}"
: "${IK_NCMOE:=17}"

# --- the two flags that do the work -----------------------------------------
# -rtr is worth +25 % prefill (§11.1) and also buys the VRAM headroom noted above.
# It was suspect 3 in the NaN bisect and came back NEGATIVE (crash 5, 2026-08-13:
# 188 265 prefilled tokens without it, squarely inside the 94k-295k band), so it
# is restored.
: "${IK_RTR:=1}"
# 1024, and this is a stability decision, not a tuning one. It costs 3-5 % of
# prefill (462.9 vs 485.3 tok/s at 32k, 409.6 vs 422.7 at 128k -- RESULTS §18).
#
# -ub 2048 is implicated in the intermittent NaN-logits abort (TODO item 9).
# SIX aborts out of six under interactive traffic, none at a smaller micro-batch:
#
#   -ub 2048, interactive   6 runs   94k-331k tokens   ALL ABORT
#   -ub 2048, synthetic     1 run    397 077           clean (but stopped, not survived)
#   -ub 1024                1 run    532 148           clean
#   -ub  512                2 runs   528 577 / 541 369 clean
#
# The two clean 512 runs were real interactive use, so 1.07 M tokens of the same
# workload that aborts six times at 2048 pass without one. The reversion test on
# 2026-08-14 is what settled it: 2048 was put back deliberately and aborted at
# 331 269 tokens, wider than the 94k-295k band seen before but the same failure.
#
# Mechanism still unknown. The 4x range in time-to-abort tracks the prefill rate,
# so the abort follows prefilled VOLUME, not uptime or request count. Next place
# to look is the partial micro-batch path -- interactive traffic is mostly short
# prompts (median 1 800 tokens here, 12 of 23 below 2048, i.e. a single partial
# micro-batch), which the benchmark's eight huge uniform prompts barely exercise.
#
# Do not raise this without a reason. -ub 4096 is faster still (§18.3) and is the
# same suspect, only more so.
: "${IK_UBATCH:=1024}"

# -b does nothing for prefill here (the compute buffer tracks -ub, not -b), and
# a -ub above -b is silently clamped -- so this has to stay >= IK_UBATCH.
: "${IK_BATCH:=4096}"

# MLA cache is an already-compressed latent: f16, never q8_0. Quantizing it frees
# VRAM but --fit then needs a margin big enough for the compute buffer and gives
# most of it straight back (§10.2, net +2 %).
: "${IK_CTK:=f16}"
: "${IK_CTV:=f16}"

# --- context state layers: back ON, all three bisect steps came back negative --
# Steps 1-3 of the NaN bisect (TODO item 9) each removed one thing and each
# crashed anyway, at a prefill volume inside the same band:
#
#   step 1  --cache-ram 0   crash 3   94 372 prefilled tokens
#   step 2  -ctx-ckpt 0     crash 4  134 124
#   step 3  -rtr off        crash 5  188 265
#
# The prompt cache, the context checkpoints and the run-time repack are therefore
# all exonerated, and the flags that disabled them are gone -- keeping them would
# cost §11.3's free re-send for nothing. The bisect continues on the --fit profile
# (serve-deepseek-v4-flash-mxfp4-gpu-cpu-128k.sh), which shares none of this
# profile's tuning.
: "${IK_EXTRA_ARGS:=-mla 3 -fidx --reasoning-format deepseek -nkvo}"
